% Regression: holding a form's description reaches for machinery by name
% -- the recorder a drawing is taken by, the placement that puts one on
% the page, and the replay that paints one into a device that paints.
% Every one of those is a dot-named operator relocated out of systemdict
% into .internaldict, and a name that cannot be found there is undefined
% at the first form rather than at load. This runs a form twice, so both
% the taking and the placing are reached, and either failing is an error
% rather than a page that quietly took the other path.
%
% Run through the ppm device by run-ps-test.sh, and it has to be a device
% a drawing can be held for: one declaring the five marking methods, a
% colour space a record holds, no path filling and no screen. A run on a
% device that paints nothing takes the uncached path and reaches none of
% what this exists to guard. Prints SUCCESS iff a cacheable form runs
% through execform without error.

/faildict 1 dict def faildict /n 0 put
/assert { % bool (name) . -
    exch { pop }{ (FAIL: )print print (\n)print faildict /n 2 copy get 1 add put } ifelse
} bind def

/F <<
    /FormType 1
    /BBox [0 0 120 120]
    /Matrix [1 0 0 1 0 0]
    /PaintProc {
        pop
        0 0 120 120 rectfill
        1 setgray 20 20 80 80 rectfill
    }
>> def

% execform the same form twice: the first pass populates the form cache (the
% path that builds the raster device and clears it with the rect-fill
% operators), the second pass replays from the cache. Neither may error.
{ gsave F execform grestore
  gsave F execform grestore } stopped not
    (a cacheable form runs through execform without error) assert
% --- what a cache is for, which nothing above asks ---
% Holding that a form raises no error says nothing about the cache: one
% that is never consulted raises none either, so no such case can tell a
% description run once from one run at every placement -- which is the
% only difference a cache makes. So count the runs.
%
% The placements walk the page's edges as well as its middle. The capture
% paints under a box reaching one pixel beyond the form on every side, so
% a form against the low edge starts that box at a negative coordinate
% where the page cuts it; a cut counted against the description rather
% than against the box reads as a form painting outside itself, and such
% a form is captured and then painted afresh at every placement -- which
% costs more than holding nothing at all.
/PLACEMENTS 5 def
/runsat { % x y (label) . -
    /RL exch def /RY exch def /RX exch def
    /runs 0 def
    /RF << /FormType 1 /BBox [0 0 20 20] /Matrix matrix
           /PaintProc { pop /runs runs 1 add store 0 0 20 20 rectfill } >> def
    0 1 PLACEMENTS 1 sub
        { pop gsave RX RY translate RF execform grestore } for
    runs PLACEMENTS lt RL assert
    runs 0 gt RL assert
} bind def

50.5  200.5 (a form placed in the middle of the page is held) runsat
0.5   200.5 (a form placed against the left edge is held) runsat
200.5 0.5   (a form placed against the bottom edge is held) runsat
0.5   0.5   (a form placed in the low corner is held) runsat
560.5 700.5 (a form placed against the far edges is held) runsat


% A form whose paint escapes the box it declared cannot be held: what a
% drawing carried would differ from what painting it afresh does. The
% cache records that with an entry naming no drawing, so the description
% is not run a second time for the same answer. Giving the cache up has
% to pass over such an entry rather than reach for a drawing that was
% never put there -- and the cache is given up where the page device is
% replaced, which is an ordinary thing for a program to do.
/G <<
    /FormType 1
    /BBox [0 0 20 20]
    /Matrix [1 0 0 1 0 0]
    /PaintProc { pop 0 0 200 200 rectfill }
>> def

{ gsave 100 100 translate G execform grestore
  << /PageSize [612 792] >> setpagedevice } stopped not
    (the page device is replaced after a form that could not be held) assert

faildict /n get 0 eq { (SUCCESS\n) print } { faildict /n get =print ( failures\n) print } ifelse
flush
quit
